  {
    "episode_id": 72838,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72838.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "well uh... let me just first say that i don't believe that the congressman was was removed from the committee because he voted against the farm bill i think that was a wholly different situation with a different message uh... but more importantly you're right but there is has been a lot of discussion that uh... that the SNAP pieces or the nutrition pieces need to be separated from the farm bill and look that differently. But because it is not that way right now, the only way that we can address the situation is to vote against it. Yes, there are people that think that it wasn't enough cuts, but let me just suggest to you this. The Senate only cut $4.5 billion out of there in their bill for SNAP. We turn around and do $16 billion. When you look at the fact that almost 8% of the US population is unemployed, and that the current poverty rate is the second highest has been in 45 years. You can't talk about not feeding hungry children and hungry families. And when you look at the effects of SNAP, about 75% of all SNAP recipients are families with young children. And the other 25% are seniors or people with disabilities. So what is it that we are to do? Are we to say that as a nation, We are going to refuse to feed hungry people. We just say as a nation that we are not concerned about the nutrition of our children. I don't think that people understand the consequences of making those kinds of decisions unless they live in environments like mine.",
