    "turn_text": "access to either public transportation or an automobile, what you do is you go to these What we call corner stores or you go to places where the food is not healthy or more importantly what you do is you go to fast food restaurants where the nutrition value is meal and the calorie count and the fat count are way up. That's one of the issues which is what we call food death if you live in some of these environments and it happens not just in cities it happens in rural communities as well. When you look at the fact that most poor children and many rural children as well, urban and rural, get 90% of their nutrition from schools. So if their school meals are not nutritious, then they are not going to be healthy. You also look at the fact that we cut back on education funding. One of the very first things they cut back on is physical education. So you don't have young people who are being physically fit because they are not getting exercise. In environments like mine, there are children who don't even walk to school for fear of their own lives. So how do you say that the two are not connected? I further want to say that obesity is an epidemic in this country. We",
